
  Remove the worktrees done with, their local branches and their meta records. The remote branch is never touched.
  A worktree is done with when its pull request merged or was closed while its branch stands at
  the pull request's head (a commit made since keeps it), or when origin deleted the branch it
  tracks. --merged, --closed and --gone pick among these reasons; none given asks for all three.
  Origin is fetched first for --gone, unless --no-fetch. Never the main worktree nor the base branch.

  Without arguments, every worktree done with that is safe to remove is pruned; the wizard lists
  them all, the safe ones checked. Name worktrees to prune only those.

  A worktree that is locked, has uncommitted changes, holds commits no other branch nor remote
  holds or has an open pull request is unsafe. One not named is kept, and named with
  the command that prunes it anyway; one named refuses the whole run before anything is removed,
  unless --force. --force lifts that refusal only: it still asks to confirm, unless --yes.

  Each worktree is removed in turn: the jobs it runs stopped first (one that will not stop keeps
  it, unless --force), then its on_clean hooks: a failing hook keeps it. A failure
  does not stop the others. The children a removal leaves behind stay on their removed parent,
  named with the `wtm reparent` that moves them, unless --reparent-children moves them onto the
  nearest worktree that stays, else the base branch.

  The data a worktree made in shared services (a database, a bucket) is dropped once it is
  removed, its claims let go of after the last removal. A service that is down cannot drop it:
  it is owed until the service next starts, unless --drop-data starts the service to drop it now.
  --keep-data keeps it all. prune first drops what earlier removals owe a service that is up.

  Usage:
    wtm-next prune [worktree...] [flags]

  Examples:
    # Prune every worktree done with that is safe to remove
    wtm prune

    # Only those whose pull request merged; their children move onto the nearest survivor
    wtm prune --merged --reparent-children

    # A worktree kept for being unsafe, pruned anyway
    wtm prune feat/login --force

    # See the plan first, then apply exactly that plan
    wtm prune --dry-run
    wtm prune --expect-fingerprint <hash>

    # For a script or an agent, with the v2 JSON envelope
    wtm prune --output json

  Flags:
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
        --closed                      Prune the worktrees whose pull request was closed unmerged
        --drop-data                   Start a shared service that is down to drop the namespaces the pruned worktrees made in it now, rather than once it next starts
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
        --force                       Prune a worktree that is locked, has uncommitted changes, unpushed commits or an open pull request, or whose jobs would not stop
        --gone                        Prune the worktrees whose branch origin deleted
    -h, --help                        help for prune
        --keep-data                   Keep the namespaces the pruned worktrees made in shared services (databases, buckets): nothing is dropped
        --merged                      Prune the worktrees whose pull request merged (none of --merged, --closed, --gone asks for all three)
        --no-fetch                    Read origin's branches as last fetched, for --gone
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
        --reparent-children           Move the children a pruned worktree leaves behind onto its nearest surviving ancestor, else the base branch
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation (every worktree done with that is safe to remove unless worktrees are named; children stay orphaned unless --reparent-children; an unsafe worktree named still refuses the run unless --force)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

